vm: let a port place the guest itself

Two hosts already cannot put the Macintosh where SheepShaver's fixed
addresses ask for it, and upstream has an answer for each: MEM_BULK makes
VMBaseDiff a variable so the block can land anywhere, and the Apple
x86_64 build banks Low Memory and the Kernel Data into two static arrays
so their addresses — 1.6 GB apart — need not be covered by the mapping at
all.

A bare-metal port needs both at once, and neither of the existing symbols
fits: MEM_BULK also selects a Windows-shaped allocator with a 1.5 GB
reservation, and the Apple condition is a processor test. So this adds a
third way in, named for what it means rather than for who uses it.

With VM_PORT_PLACES_GUEST defined, VMBaseDiff is a variable the port sets
and both regions are banked. It also routes everything outside
that block to a page of its own, which is the same problem once more: a
port that cannot trap has to send those accesses somewhere, and past the
end of the block is not somewhere.

That is not a shortcut. It is what "ignoresegv" already does on hosts
that trap, and both emulators default it to true — the Macintosh's device
space and the odd stray access are absorbed there too. The count and the first
address reached are exported, so a port can report them rather than
ignore them.

Everything else is unchanged; no existing build sees any difference.

Okapia (docs/topics/engine-powerpc.md). Offered upstream as it stands: it
generalises a case the tree already answers twice.

---
diff --git a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/vm.hpp b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/vm.hpp
index e60bc85d..9eeaca55 100755
--- a/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/vm.hpp
+++ b/SheepShaver/src/kpx_cpu/src/cpu/vm.hpp
@@ -190,7 +190,7 @@ typedef uintptr vm_addr_t;
 #if REAL_ADDRESSING
 const uintptr VMBaseDiff = 0;
 #elif DIRECT_ADDRESSING
-#ifdef MEM_BULK
+#if defined(MEM_BULK) || defined(VM_PORT_PLACES_GUEST)
 extern uintptr VMBaseDiff;
 #elif defined(NATMEM_OFFSET)
 const uintptr VMBaseDiff = NATMEM_OFFSET;
@@ -208,12 +208,25 @@ const uintptr VMBaseDiff = NATMEM_OFFSET;
 static inline uint8 * vm_do_get_real_address(vm_addr_t addr)
 {
 	uintptr a = vm_wrap_address(addr);
-#if defined(__APPLE__) && defined(__x86_64__) || defined(MEM_BULK)
+#if defined(__APPLE__) && defined(__x86_64__) || defined(MEM_BULK) || defined(VM_PORT_PLACES_GUEST)
 	extern uint8 gZeroPage[0x3000], gKernelData[0x2000];
 #ifndef MEM_BULK
 	if (a < 0x3000) return &gZeroPage[a];
 #endif
 	if ((a & ~0x1fff) == 0x68ffe000 || (a & ~0x1fff) == 0x5fffe000) return &gKernelData[a & 0x1fff];
+#endif
+#ifdef VM_PORT_PLACES_GUEST
+	// Anything outside the block the port placed: the Macintosh's device space,
+	// and the strays that "ignoresegv" absorbs on a host that traps — which is
+	// the default in both emulators, so absorbing is upstream's behaviour and
+	// not a shortcut. One unsigned compare, and the count is there to be read
+	// rather than to be ignored.
+	extern uint32 gGuestLow, gGuestSize, gStrayCount, gStrayFirst;
+	extern uint8 gStrayPage[0x1000];
+	if ((uint32)(a - gGuestLow) >= gGuestSize) {
+		if (gStrayCount++ == 0) gStrayFirst = a;
+		return &gStrayPage[a & 0xfff];
+	}
 #endif
 	return (uint8 *)(VMBaseDiff + a);
 }
